  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Areas of frost tonight, then more widespread frost \nMonday night with a potential for a freeze. \n\nSurface high pressure, centered over western Ontario Canada tonight, \nwill drive a cooler airmass over our region. This will bring frost \nconcerns for tonight...then more widespread frost...or even a freeze \nfor the hills of southwestern NYS and the eastern Lake Ontario, for \nMonday night as the surface high crest over our region. \n\nThere is a possible hindrance to the frost formation tonight \nthough...this being low clouds/moisture over the western Great Lakes \nthis afternoon that will stream eastward over our region later \ntonight. If this moisture yields enough low clouds frost may be \nminimal, especially for SW NYS,...but will side on the note of \ncaution and place frost advisories out for our traditionally colder \nSW NYS counties as well as the eastern Lake Ontario region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returns by the middle of the week \nwith periods of rain Tuesday night through Thursday.\n\nThis afternoon a shortwave trough, seen on water vapor imagery, is \nnearing the PAC NW, with this featuring sliding down a building west \ncoast ridge axis early this week...forming a deep, nearly closed off \nupper level low over the eastern Great Lakes Tuesday night into \nWednesday. \n\nMoisture will stream northward from the Gulf ahead of this feature, \nthough the deeper moisture will remain to our south over the Ohio \nValley. Still Pwats rising towards just over an inch will fuel a \nperiod of rain...first Tuesday night along a warm front and then \nWednesday - Wednesday night along a passing cold front. \n\nInstability is low, though a rumble of thunder along the cold front \nis possible for Wednesday afternoon and evening. \n\nOverall rainfall with this system is forecasted to range between a \nhalf to one inch, and model ensemble river forecasts all maintain \nforecasted gauges below flood stage. There is potential for over an \ninch of rain in a 24-hour period as per the grand ensemble guidance \nfrom the GEFS, GEPS and ENS which display nearly a 1 in 5 chance for \nover an inch of rain over the upslope southern Tug Hill region \nWednesday and Wednesday night.\n\nWhile the steadier rain will be pushing to our east Wednesday night \nwrap around moisture within the passage of the cool upper level \ntrough axis will support a wealth of clouds yielding light showers \nthrough the day Thursday.",
